WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.211
Lesson three point one.

2
00:00:01.517 --> 00:00:03.655
What a model actually is: numbers,

3
00:00:03.655 --> 00:00:04.724
multiplied and added.

4
00:00:08.693 --> 00:00:11.203
From lesson one point two: at each slot

5
00:00:11.203 --> 00:00:13.602
the model is handed five numbers, and it

6
00:00:13.602 --> 00:00:14.606
answers with nine.

7
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.227
Inside, the smallest working part is a

8
00:00:17.227 --> 00:00:19.849
neuron: some numbers in, one number out.

9
00:00:20.417 --> 00:00:22.356
Here are three inputs, made up for

10
00:00:22.356 --> 00:00:24.716
illustration, with names borrowed from our

11
00:00:24.716 --> 00:00:27.284
five: a pluck, how loud it was, and how

12
00:00:27.284 --> 00:00:28.962
busy the guitar has been lately.

13
00:00:29.431 --> 00:00:31.626
Each input travels along its own line, and

14
00:00:31.626 --> 00:00:33.675
each line carries a weight: a number

15
00:00:33.675 --> 00:00:35.870
saying how much that input matters, and in

16
00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:36.650
which direction.

17
00:00:36.943 --> 00:00:38.797
A weight of two makes the pluck count

18
00:00:38.797 --> 00:00:39.138
double.

19
00:00:39.431 --> 00:00:41.431
A half makes the loudness count for half.

20
00:00:41.723 --> 00:00:43.723
Minus one means a busy guitar pushes the

21
00:00:43.723 --> 00:00:44.309
answer down.

22
00:00:44.601 --> 00:00:46.845
A weight of zero would mean: ignore this

23
00:00:46.845 --> 00:00:47.138
input.

24
00:00:47.584 --> 00:00:49.740
The neuron multiplies each input by its

25
00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.465
weight, and adds the three up.

26
00:00:51.788 --> 00:00:54.160
Then it adds one more number, the bias,

27
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.262
which is there whatever the inputs are.

28
00:00:56.586 --> 00:00:58.796
It sets where the neuron sits when every

29
00:00:58.796 --> 00:00:59.551
input is zero.

30
00:01:00.225 --> 00:01:02.643
Read it left to right: weight one times

31
00:01:02.643 --> 00:01:04.955
input one, weight two times input two,

32
00:01:04.955 --> 00:01:07.266
weight three times input three, plus the

33
00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:07.803
bias, b.

34
00:01:08.126 --> 00:01:09.362
The answer is called z.

35
00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:10.757
Now the numbers.

36
00:01:11.074 --> 00:01:12.187
Two times one is two.

37
00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:14.731
A half times nought point eight is nought

38
00:01:14.731 --> 00:01:15.314
point four.

39
00:01:15.632 --> 00:01:17.910
Minus one times nought point four is minus

40
00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:18.864
nought point four.

41
00:01:19.182 --> 00:01:20.719
And the bias is minus a half.

42
00:01:21.037 --> 00:01:23.051
Add them up: z is one and a half.

43
00:01:23.521 --> 00:01:24.282
Why this shape?

44
00:01:24.587 --> 00:01:26.669
A weighted sum lets every input count by

45
00:01:26.669 --> 00:01:28.498
its own amount, with its own sign.

46
00:01:28.802 --> 00:01:30.935
And it is smooth: nudge one weight a

47
00:01:30.935 --> 00:01:33.322
little, say from two to two point one, and

48
00:01:33.322 --> 00:01:35.506
z moves a little, from one and a half to

49
00:01:35.506 --> 00:01:36.217
one point six.

50
00:01:36.521 --> 00:01:38.502
Lesson three point three needs exactly

51
00:01:38.502 --> 00:01:38.756
that.

52
00:01:39.181 --> 00:01:41.263
The weights and the bias are this neuron's

53
00:01:41.263 --> 00:01:43.298
parameters: the numbers it can learn.

54
00:01:43.588 --> 00:01:44.460
This one has four.

55
00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.778
Put a second neuron beside the first.

56
00:01:50.087 --> 00:01:52.305
It reads the same three inputs, with its

57
00:01:52.305 --> 00:01:54.471
own weights and its own bias, and gives

58
00:01:54.471 --> 00:01:56.431
its own answer: nought point six.

59
00:01:56.871 --> 00:01:59.503
Written for both neurons at once, this is

60
00:01:59.503 --> 00:02:00.699
y equals W x plus b.

61
00:02:01.058 --> 00:02:04.049
W is a matrix, a grid of numbers: each row

62
00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:05.664
holds one neuron's weights.

63
00:02:06.023 --> 00:02:08.475
x is the column of inputs, b holds one

64
00:02:08.475 --> 00:02:10.987
bias per neuron, and y is the column of

65
00:02:10.987 --> 00:02:12.542
answers, one per neuron.

66
00:02:13.113 --> 00:02:15.820
Row one across, times the column down: two

67
00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.201
times one, plus a half times nought point

68
00:02:18.201 --> 00:02:20.150
eight, and so on, plus its bias.

69
00:02:20.474 --> 00:02:22.856
The same sum as before: one and a half.

70
00:02:23.181 --> 00:02:24.859
Row two gives nought point six.

71
00:02:25.339 --> 00:02:27.584
This is a linear layer: many neurons

72
00:02:27.584 --> 00:02:28.867
reading the same inputs.

73
00:02:29.187 --> 00:02:31.646
Its shapes: W has one row per output and

74
00:02:31.646 --> 00:02:33.624
one column per input, so out by in.

75
00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:34.907
Here two by three.

76
00:02:35.228 --> 00:02:37.526
Counting its parameters: out times in

77
00:02:37.526 --> 00:02:38.969
weights, plus out biases.

78
00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.268
Two times three plus two: eight.

79
00:02:42.804 --> 00:02:43.605
Now a real one.

80
00:02:43.925 --> 00:02:46.061
This is our transformer, building its

81
00:02:46.061 --> 00:02:46.434
layers.

82
00:02:46.755 --> 00:02:48.783
The dimmed middle lines are boxes for

83
00:02:48.783 --> 00:02:49.371
topic four.

84
00:02:49.691 --> 00:02:52.360
The last line is its head: n n dot Linear,

85
00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.816
PyTorch's linear layer, from d model, the

86
00:02:54.816 --> 00:02:57.165
width of the core, ninety-six numbers per

87
00:02:57.165 --> 00:02:59.194
slot, to n voices, the nine drums.

88
00:02:59.635 --> 00:03:01.986
So W is nine by ninety-six, x is the

89
00:03:01.986 --> 00:03:04.699
core's ninety-six numbers, and b and y are

90
00:03:04.699 --> 00:03:05.302
nine long.

91
00:03:05.663 --> 00:03:08.256
Its parameters: nine times ninety-six

92
00:03:08.256 --> 00:03:10.848
weights is eight hundred and sixty-four,

93
00:03:10.848 --> 00:03:13.139
plus nine biases, eight hundred and

94
00:03:13.139 --> 00:03:13.983
seventy-three.

95
00:03:14.515 --> 00:03:16.671
The first line is a linear layer too,

96
00:03:16.671 --> 00:03:19.204
going the other way: from our five inputs

97
00:03:19.204 --> 00:03:20.174
out to ninety-six.

98
00:03:20.498 --> 00:03:22.330
That's ninety-six times five plus

99
00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:24.648
ninety-six, five hundred and seventy-six

100
00:03:24.648 --> 00:03:25.241
parameters.

101
00:03:25.564 --> 00:03:27.774
The LSTM's and S4D's heads read a core a

102
00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:30.199
hundred and twenty-eight wide, so each has

103
00:03:30.199 --> 00:03:31.762
eleven hundred and sixty-one.

104
00:03:35.243 --> 00:03:37.015
Why not just stack linear layers?

105
00:03:37.337 --> 00:03:39.753
Take our neuron as layer one, and call its

106
00:03:39.753 --> 00:03:40.236
answer h.

107
00:03:40.558 --> 00:03:43.028
Add a second layer that reads h: three h

108
00:03:43.028 --> 00:03:43.511
plus one.

109
00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:44.531
Also made up.

110
00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:46.670
Put layer one inside layer two.

111
00:03:46.976 --> 00:03:49.370
Multiply out: six x one, plus one and a

112
00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:51.662
half x two, minus three x three, minus a

113
00:03:51.662 --> 00:03:51.917
half.

114
00:03:52.222 --> 00:03:54.464
Check with our inputs: through the two

115
00:03:54.464 --> 00:03:56.705
layers, h is one and a half and z is five

116
00:03:56.705 --> 00:03:57.266
and a half.

117
00:03:57.571 --> 00:03:59.915
Straight from the new weights: also five

118
00:03:59.915 --> 00:04:00.475
and a half.

119
00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.937
Two layers, but it's one neuron in

120
00:04:02.937 --> 00:04:03.428
disguise.

121
00:04:03.755 --> 00:04:06.045
The same is true for whole layers: a

122
00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:08.116
matrix times a matrix is just another

123
00:04:08.116 --> 00:04:08.498
matrix.

124
00:04:08.825 --> 00:04:11.224
However many linear layers you stack, the

125
00:04:11.224 --> 00:04:13.677
result is one linear layer: it can only

126
00:04:13.677 --> 00:04:14.713
ever weigh and add.

127
00:04:16.281 --> 00:04:18.819
The fix is an activation: a fixed bend,

128
00:04:18.819 --> 00:04:21.141
applied to each neuron's answer before the

129
00:04:21.141 --> 00:04:22.221
next layer reads it.

130
00:04:22.545 --> 00:04:24.813
The simplest is ReLU: keep z if it's

131
00:04:24.813 --> 00:04:26.541
positive, otherwise give zero.

132
00:04:26.865 --> 00:04:29.457
Another is tanh, the hyperbolic tangent:

133
00:04:29.457 --> 00:04:31.779
an S-shaped curve that squashes any z into

134
00:04:31.779 --> 00:04:33.237
the range minus one to one.

135
00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:36.108
Our neuron's one and a half goes through

136
00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:36.986
ReLU unchanged.

137
00:04:37.337 --> 00:04:39.560
Minus nought point seven comes out as

138
00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:39.852
zero.

139
00:04:40.203 --> 00:04:42.485
Through tanh, one and a half becomes

140
00:04:42.485 --> 00:04:44.766
nought point nine oh five, and minus

141
00:04:44.766 --> 00:04:47.106
nought point seven becomes minus nought

142
00:04:47.106 --> 00:04:48.159
point six oh four.

143
00:04:48.675 --> 00:04:50.822
Now put a ReLU between our two layers.

144
00:04:51.161 --> 00:04:53.760
For every h below zero, z is just one, so

145
00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.190
the two layers no longer multiply out into

146
00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:56.416
one.

147
00:04:56.755 --> 00:04:59.184
That bend is what lets a network say "only

148
00:04:59.184 --> 00:05:01.896
if": only if the guitar is loud, only if

149
00:05:01.896 --> 00:05:02.518
it is busy.

150
00:05:02.857 --> 00:05:04.496
Stacking now means something.

151
00:05:06.096 --> 00:05:07.370
Which does our code use?

152
00:05:07.688 --> 00:05:10.236
The S4D and transformer models use GELU: a

153
00:05:10.236 --> 00:05:10.979
smoothed ReLU.

154
00:05:11.297 --> 00:05:13.473
It is z times the chance that a standard

155
00:05:13.473 --> 00:05:15.756
bell-curve number falls below z, so it's

156
00:05:15.756 --> 00:05:17.826
near zero for negatives and near z for

157
00:05:17.826 --> 00:05:18.356
positives.

158
00:05:18.675 --> 00:05:20.957
GELU sits inside every S4D block, and in

159
00:05:20.957 --> 00:05:22.868
the transformer's feed-forward part.

160
00:05:23.186 --> 00:05:25.468
tanh sits inside the LSTM, which stays a

161
00:05:25.468 --> 00:05:26.583
box until topic four.

162
00:05:26.901 --> 00:05:29.343
ReLU is in no drum model; semester one's

163
00:05:29.343 --> 00:05:30.670
pitch classifier used it.

164
00:05:30.988 --> 00:05:33.536
And the head has none: its nine numbers go

165
00:05:33.536 --> 00:05:33.960
out raw.

166
00:05:34.279 --> 00:05:36.826
Our two test numbers through GELU: one and

167
00:05:36.826 --> 00:05:39.055
a half becomes one point four oh, minus

168
00:05:39.055 --> 00:05:41.178
nought point seven becomes minus nought

169
00:05:41.178 --> 00:05:42.028
point one seven.

170
00:05:45.473 --> 00:05:47.088
Now stack layers into a network.

171
00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:48.550
Here is our drum model.

172
00:05:48.853 --> 00:05:51.022
Each orange box is a stage: the input

173
00:05:51.022 --> 00:05:53.292
layer and the head are one layer each, and

174
00:05:53.292 --> 00:05:54.150
the core is many.

175
00:05:54.452 --> 00:05:56.621
A layer is one transformation that takes a

176
00:05:56.621 --> 00:05:58.337
list of numbers and gives another.

177
00:05:58.639 --> 00:06:00.405
The five inputs for a slot come in.

178
00:06:00.707 --> 00:06:02.776
An input layer widens them to the core's

179
00:06:02.776 --> 00:06:05.348
width, d: ninety-six for the transformer,

180
00:06:05.348 --> 00:06:07.114
a hundred and twenty-eight for S4D.

181
00:06:07.416 --> 00:06:09.081
The LSTM reads the five directly.

182
00:06:09.384 --> 00:06:11.351
Then the core: the LSTM, S4D or

183
00:06:11.351 --> 00:06:13.268
transformer, a box until topic four.

184
00:06:13.571 --> 00:06:15.689
Then the head, the linear layer we just

185
00:06:15.689 --> 00:06:17.707
counted, with a norm before it in the

186
00:06:17.707 --> 00:06:20.078
transformer, also topic four, and out come

187
00:06:20.078 --> 00:06:21.541
nine numbers, one per drum.

188
00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:24.521
Running the inputs through every layer in

189
00:06:24.521 --> 00:06:27.217
order, to the output, is the forward pass.

190
00:06:27.569 --> 00:06:29.327
Live, it runs once per slot.

191
00:06:29.679 --> 00:06:32.609
In training, a whole chunk at once: x of B

192
00:06:32.609 --> 00:06:35.246
songs by C slots by five goes in, and B by

193
00:06:35.246 --> 00:06:36.418
C by nine comes out.

194
00:06:38.002 --> 00:06:38.745
What comes out?

195
00:06:39.042 --> 00:06:41.122
Here is the real transformer drummer, T

196
00:06:41.122 --> 00:06:43.251
one twenty-eight, at its epoch eleven,

197
00:06:43.251 --> 00:06:45.578
handed a made-up guitar: a pluck on every

198
00:06:45.578 --> 00:06:47.509
beat at a hundred and twenty beats per

199
00:06:47.509 --> 00:06:47.856
minute.

200
00:06:48.153 --> 00:06:49.886
These are its nine numbers at slot

201
00:06:49.886 --> 00:06:50.975
sixteen, a downbeat.

202
00:06:51.273 --> 00:06:53.451
Each is called a logit: the raw output

203
00:06:53.451 --> 00:06:55.135
number, any size, either sign.

204
00:06:55.599 --> 00:06:58.004
Bigger means leaning towards a hit: the

205
00:06:58.004 --> 00:07:00.088
kick, at one point two seven, on a

206
00:07:00.088 --> 00:07:00.569
downbeat.

207
00:07:00.889 --> 00:07:02.973
The high tom, at minus six point one

208
00:07:02.973 --> 00:07:04.736
eight, is leaning hard against.

209
00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:07.248
How a logit becomes a probability is the

210
00:07:07.248 --> 00:07:07.889
next lesson.

211
00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:12.379
What does learning change?

212
00:07:12.621 --> 00:07:14.638
Not the code: the shapes, the order of the

213
00:07:14.638 --> 00:07:16.333
layers and the activations are fixed when

214
00:07:16.333 --> 00:07:17.099
the model is built.

215
00:07:17.341 --> 00:07:19.157
Only the numbers: every weight, every

216
00:07:19.157 --> 00:07:20.731
bias, and every other learned number

217
00:07:20.731 --> 00:07:21.376
inside the core.

218
00:07:21.618 --> 00:07:23.393
In our trainer, the optimiser, the part

219
00:07:23.393 --> 00:07:25.047
that changes them, is handed model dot

220
00:07:25.047 --> 00:07:26.298
parameters, and nothing else.

221
00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:28.315
By default that's this second line, plain

222
00:07:28.315 --> 00:07:29.768
Adam: lesson three point three.

223
00:07:31.246 --> 00:07:32.150
So how many numbers?

224
00:07:32.422 --> 00:07:34.366
Counted with our own code: each model

225
00:07:34.366 --> 00:07:36.355
built on the CPU from its run's settings,

226
00:07:36.355 --> 00:07:38.074
adding up the size of every parameter.

227
00:07:38.345 --> 00:07:40.334
The LSTM, a hundred and twenty-eight wide

228
00:07:40.334 --> 00:07:42.143
in two layers: two hundred and two

229
00:07:42.143 --> 00:07:44.133
thousand, three hundred and seventy-seven.

230
00:07:44.404 --> 00:07:46.303
S4D, a hundred and twenty-eight wide in

231
00:07:46.303 --> 00:07:48.564
four blocks: two hundred and one thousand,

232
00:07:48.564 --> 00:07:49.513
six hundred and nine.

233
00:07:49.785 --> 00:07:51.684
The transformer, ninety-six wide in two

234
00:07:51.684 --> 00:07:53.492
blocks: two hundred and twenty-six

235
00:07:53.492 --> 00:07:55.391
thousand, eight hundred and fifty-seven.

236
00:07:55.663 --> 00:07:57.562
The Rock, Jazz and Country drummers are

237
00:07:57.562 --> 00:07:59.416
the same model, trained on other songs.

238
00:07:59.687 --> 00:08:01.315
The feedback version reads fourteen

239
00:08:01.315 --> 00:08:03.530
inputs, so its input layer is bigger: two

240
00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:05.475
hundred and twenty-seven thousand, seven

241
00:08:05.475 --> 00:08:06.515
hundred and twenty-one.

242
00:08:06.888 --> 00:08:09.172
The LSTM and S4D were matched: they are

243
00:08:09.172 --> 00:08:11.202
within seven hundred and sixty-eight of

244
00:08:11.202 --> 00:08:11.760
each other.

245
00:08:12.065 --> 00:08:14.095
The transformer is about twelve percent

246
00:08:14.095 --> 00:08:16.480
bigger; say so whenever they are compared.

247
00:08:16.785 --> 00:08:18.815
And the head we counted is under nought

248
00:08:18.815 --> 00:08:20.439
point six percent of each model.

249
00:08:20.743 --> 00:08:22.773
Nearly all the numbers live in the core.

250
00:08:24.359 --> 00:08:26.561
For T one twenty-eight, that's two hundred

251
00:08:26.561 --> 00:08:28.763
and twenty-six thousand, eight hundred and

252
00:08:28.763 --> 00:08:29.741
fifty-seven numbers.

253
00:08:30.035 --> 00:08:32.188
Learning means nudging every one of them,

254
00:08:32.188 --> 00:08:34.389
a little at a time, to make a score called

255
00:08:34.389 --> 00:08:35.221
the loss smaller.

256
00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:37.423
What the loss is comes in lesson three

257
00:08:37.423 --> 00:08:39.722
point two; how to nudge, in three point

258
00:08:39.722 --> 00:08:40.016
three.

259
00:08:41.767 --> 00:08:44.144
A neuron weighs its inputs, adds them, and

260
00:08:44.144 --> 00:08:44.751
adds a bias.

261
00:08:45.054 --> 00:08:47.330
A linear layer is many neurons, y equals W

262
00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:47.785
x plus b.

263
00:08:48.088 --> 00:08:50.010
Between layers goes a bend, or they

264
00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:50.920
collapse into one.

265
00:08:51.224 --> 00:08:53.145
And learning changes only the numbers.

266
00:08:53.449 --> 00:08:55.471
This pairs with the reading tour in the

267
00:08:55.471 --> 00:08:57.747
course explainer, the rows for model dot p

268
00:08:57.747 --> 00:08:59.062
y and transformer dot p y.

269
00:08:59.365 --> 00:09:01.034
Next: a loss for nine drums.

